---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FlipCards from "../components/FlipCards.astro";
import ProblemFramework from "../components/ProblemFramework.astro";
import BeforeAfterSliders from "../components/BeforeAfterSliders.astro";
import LofiHifiFade from "../components/LofiHifiFade.astro";
import AnnotatedViewer from "../components/AnnotatedViewer.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Fishdoro — Case Study | Fitri Zahwa"
  description="A Pomodoro timer reimagined as a cozy fishing mini-game. Full case study covering research, design process, and build."
>
  <div class="cs-shell">
    <!-- Sidebar-style back nav -->
    <a href="/home" class="cs-back" aria-label="Back to portfolio">
      <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none">
        <path
          d="M10 3L5 8L10 13"
          stroke="#6b8f4e"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
      </svg>
      <span>Back</span>
    </a>

    <!-- Scrollable content container — .cs-content is the scroll root -->
    <div class="cs-content" id="csContent">
      <!-- Progress bar — first child of .cs-content -->
      <div class="cs-progress-bar" id="csProgressBar">
        <div class="cs-progress-fill" id="csProgressFill"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- Inner content — max-width centered -->
      <div class="cs-inner">
        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 1 — HEADER
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <header class="cs-header">
          <!-- Tags -->
          <div class="cs-tags">
            <span class="cs-tag">UI/UX</span>
            <span class="cs-tag">Pixel Art</span>
            <span class="cs-tag">Gamification</span>
            <span class="cs-tag">Personal Project</span>
          </div>

          <!-- Title -->
          <h1 class="cs-title">Fishdoro</h1>

          <!-- Subtitle -->
          <p class="cs-subtitle">
            A cozy Pomodoro timer disguised as a fishing game. Complete a focus
            session. Catch a fish. That's it.
          </p>

          <!-- Opening paragraphs -->
          <p class="cs-body">
            Productivity tools have a paradox: the apps designed to help you
            focus are often the reason you stop using them. Pomodoro timers are
            everywhere — minimal, functional, and nearly identical. Most of them
            work fine in theory. But people keep quitting them.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            I wanted to understand why. And then I wanted to build something
            different.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            This case study documents the research, design decisions, and build
            process behind that question.
          </p>

          <!-- Hero image -->
          <div class="cs-hero-img">
            <img
              src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/Hero.jpg"
              alt="Fishdoro — hero screenshot"
              loading="eager"
            />
          </div>

          <!-- Meta row -->
          <div class="cs-meta-row">
            <div class="cs-meta-item">
              <span class="cs-meta-label">Role</span>
              <span class="cs-meta-value">UI/UX Designer · Developer</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-meta-item">
              <span class="cs-meta-label">Type</span>
              <span class="cs-meta-value">Personal exploration project</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-meta-item">
              <span class="cs-meta-label">Stack</span>
              <span class="cs-meta-value">Electron · HTML · CSS · JS</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </header>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 2 — BACKGROUND
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section" id="background">
          <div class="cs-section-pill">Background</div>
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">The productivity paradox</h2>

          <blockquote class="cs-blockquote">
            “What if the reward was part of the focus itself?”
          </blockquote>

          <p class="cs-body">
            Most productivity apps promise to help you focus. In practice, they
            add cognitive overhead — more notifications, more dashboards, more
            decisions. The tool becomes the distraction.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            Fishdoro started with a single question: what if the reward
            mechanism was built into the timer itself — not a badge you check
            later, but something that happens in real time as you focus?
          </p>

          <h3 class="cs-subsection-title">What is Fishdoro?</h3>
          <p class="cs-body">
            Fishdoro is a Pomodoro timer reimagined as a cozy fishing mini-game.
            Every completed focus session catches a pixel fish — the reward is
            embedded in the act of focusing, not external to it. Built with
            Electron, HTML, CSS, and JavaScript — entirely by the designer.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 3 — DISCOVER
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section" id="discover">
          <div class="cs-section-pill">Discover</div>
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">
            The Process — Discover → Define → Develop
          </h2>

          <p class="cs-body">
            To understand the problem before designing a solution, I turned to
            Reddit — a space where people talk honestly about their productivity
            habits, frustrations, and tool preferences. I analyzed threads
            across three areas: general Pomodoro discussions, opinions on
            gamification in productivity apps, and user reviews of competitor
            apps like Forest.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            To make sense of the patterns, I used thematic coding — grouping
            recurring comments into themes, then assigning confidence levels
            based on how often each theme appeared across different threads.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">Three insights emerged consistently:</p>

          <!-- Flip Cards — insight reveal -->
          <p class="cs-hint">Flip each card to read the full insight ↓</p>
          <FlipCards />

          <div class="cs-bridge">
            These three patterns became the foundation for every major decision
            in Fishdoro.
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 3 — DEFINE
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section" id="define">
          <div class="cs-section-pill">Define</div>

          <p class="cs-body">
            The research pointed to one core tension: people don't quit Pomodoro
            because they lack discipline. They quit because the tools don't
            adapt to how they actually work.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            From the three insights, three design problems emerged:
          </p>

          <!-- Problem Framework + ScrollReveal -->
          <ProblemFramework />
        </section>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 4 — DEVELOP
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section" id="develop">
          <div class="cs-section-pill">Develop</div>

          <!-- 4a: Structure first — IA and user flows -->
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">Structure first — IA and user flows</h2>
          <p class="cs-body">
            Before any visual work, I mapped the entire experience as an
            Information Architecture and a set of core user flows — from
            starting a session, customizing duration, managing breaks, receiving
            fish rewards, viewing the fishing log, and adjusting settings.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            The goal was to keep navigation invisible. In a tool people use
            while trying to focus, every extra decision is friction. Every path
            through the app needed to feel obvious without thinking.
          </p>

          <!-- 4b: Visual direction -->
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">Visual direction — cozy by design</h2>
          <p class="cs-body">
            The visual language came from a simple premise: if focusing feels
            like fishing, the app should look like the world you're fishing in.
            I built a moodboard around pixel art, warm earth tones, soft light,
            and retro-game UI references — things that feel familiar and calming
            rather than clinical and optimized.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            The aim was a specific emotional register: delightful enough to make
            you smile when you open it, quiet enough to disappear once you start
            working.
          </p>

          <!-- Moodboard screenshot -->
          <div class="cs-screenshot-grid single">
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img tall">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/Moodboard.png"
                  alt="Fishdoro moodboard"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">
                Moodboard — pixel art, warm tones, retro-game UI references
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 4c: Lo-fi to hi-fi — iteration -->
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">
            Lo-fi to hi-fi — designing by iteration
          </h2>
          <p class="cs-body">
            I started with loose sketches, moved into lo-fi wireframes, and ran
            informal walkthroughs with a few people to catch early friction
            before investing in visual polish. The feedback surfaced five
            specific issues worth iterating on — inconsistent button hierarchy,
            timer customization in the wrong place, a bait icon that looked like
            a primary CTA, fish rewards that felt too random, and copy that
            broke the fishing theme.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            Each issue had a clear fix, and each fix was traceable back to a
            principle from the research: rewards should feel earned, not random.
            The interface should match the mental model users bring to it. The
            fishing theme should be consistent, not decorative.
          </p>

          <!-- Before/After Sliders — 4 pairs -->
          <BeforeAfterSliders />

          <!-- 4d: Hi-fi screens -->
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">Hi-fi screens</h2>
          <p class="cs-body">
            Once the structure was solid, I layered in all the visual elements —
            pixel art, animations, and the core cast → wait → catch loop.
          </p>

          <!-- 1. Annotated full-width timer screen -->
          <AnnotatedViewer />

          <!-- 2. Two column — home + reward -->
          <div class="cs-screenshot-grid">
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Home.png"
                  alt="Home screen"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">Home screen</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Get-Fish-Timer-Finish.png"
                  alt="Reward reveal"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">
                Reward reveal — the pixel fish
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 3. Three column — setup + session settings + app settings -->
          <div class="cs-screenshot-grid three">
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img short">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Session-Setup.png"
                  alt="Session setup"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">Session setup</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img short">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Session-Setting.png"
                  alt="Session settings"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">Session settings</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img short">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-App-Setting.png"
                  alt="App settings"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">App settings</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 4. Two column — log + report -->
          <div class="cs-screenshot-grid">
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Catch-Log.png"
                  alt="Fishing log"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">Fishing log</p>
            </div>
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Catch-Report.png"
                  alt="Catch report"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">Catch report</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 5. Two column — break state -->
          <div class="cs-screenshot-grid">
            <div class="cs-screenshot">
              <div class="cs-screenshot-img">
                <img
                  src="/assets/Fishdoro-SS/HiFi-Break-State.png"
                  alt="Break state"
                />
              </div>
              <p class="cs-screenshot-caption">
                Break state — a different pace
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- 4e: What's built so far -->
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">What's built so far</h2>
          <p class="cs-body">
            Fishdoro is currently in active development. The core experience —
            home screen, session selection, focus timer, reward reveal, and
            report — is functional. The fishing logic, transitions, and reward
            reveal work as intended, and the experience already feels
            meaningfully different from a standard Pomodoro timer.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            What remains is the craft layer: completing the full pixel art asset
            set, refining animations, and polishing transitions. These are not
            design unknowns — the decisions are made, the execution is in
            progress.
          </p>

          <!-- 4f: The build as a design signal -->
          <h2 class="cs-section-title">The build as a design signal</h2>
          <p class="cs-body">
            One aspect of this project worth noting: Fishdoro is being built
            entirely by the designer, using Electron, HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
            This was never the plan — it emerged from necessity and curiosity.
            But it changed how I design.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            When you're the one implementing your own decisions, you stop making
            design choices that are easy to draw but hard to build. You develop
            instincts for the gap between how something looks in Figma and how
            it feels when you actually interact with it. That gap, it turns out,
            is where most of the interesting design problems live.
          </p>

          <!-- Lo-fi vs Hi-fi fade comparison -->
          <LofiHifiFade />
        </section>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 5 — VALIDATION
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section" id="validation">
          <div class="cs-section-pill">Validation</div>

          <p class="cs-body">
            The current build has not yet gone through structured usability
            testing with external users. The informal walkthroughs during lo-fi
            helped shape the structure, but a proper validation round is planned
            once the full visual layer is complete.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            The key questions I want to answer: Does the fishing metaphor land
            immediately, or does it need onboarding? Does the reward frequency
            feel satisfying or distracting during actual work sessions? And does
            the cozy aesthetic hold up across longer sessions, or does it become
            visual noise?
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            These are hypotheses, not conclusions — and that's the honest state
            of this project right now.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 6 — KEY TAKEAWAYS
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section" id="takeaways">
          <div class="cs-section-pill">Key Takeaways</div>

          <div class="cs-takeaway-grid">
            <div class="cs-takeaway">
              <p class="cs-takeaway-num">01</p>
              <h3 class="cs-takeaway-title">
                The problem is more interesting than the solution.
              </h3>
              <p class="cs-body">
                The most valuable part of this project wasn't designing the
                fishing mechanic — it was discovering that people quit Pomodoro
                not because of laziness, but because of rigidity. That reframe
                changed everything. Instead of asking "how do I make a timer
                more fun?", the real question became "how do I make a timer that
                gets out of the user's way?" The difference between those two
                questions produced a fundamentally different design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="cs-takeaway">
              <p class="cs-takeaway-num">02</p>
              <h3 class="cs-takeaway-title">
                Designing something you build yourself changes how you think.
              </h3>
              <p class="cs-body">
                Building Fishdoro in code taught me that design decisions have
                weight. A button position that takes five seconds to move in
                Figma might take an hour to re-implement in the actual interface
                — and that asymmetry forces you to be more intentional early. I
                started catching interaction problems in wireframes that I
                previously would have only noticed in prototypes, because I
                could mentally simulate how they'd feel to implement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="cs-takeaway">
              <p class="cs-takeaway-num">03</p>
              <h3 class="cs-takeaway-title">
                Gamification is a hypothesis, not a feature.
              </h3>
              <p class="cs-body">
                Going into this project, I assumed that a fishing metaphor would
                naturally make focusing more enjoyable. What I learned from
                early walkthroughs is that the metaphor only works if the reward
                feels earned, not just given. The moment a fish appeared too
                easily, it stopped feeling like a reward and started feeling
                like noise. That's a principle I'd apply to any project
                involving behavioral mechanics: the value of a reward is
                inseparable from the effort that precedes it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="cs-takeaway">
              <p class="cs-takeaway-num">04</p>
              <h3 class="cs-takeaway-title">
                Knowing what you don't know is a design skill.
              </h3>
              <p class="cs-body">
                Fishdoro hasn't gone through structured usability testing yet. I
                know the questions I need to answer — does the metaphor land?
                does the reward frequency work in real sessions? — but I don't
                have the answers yet. Sitting with that uncertainty honestly,
                rather than filling it with assumptions, is something this
                project taught me to do better.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="cs-divider" />

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             SECTION 7 — SIGNING OFF
        ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="cs-section cs-signing-off" id="signoff">
          <p class="cs-body">
            The question that started Fishdoro was simple: can a boring timer
            become something you actually want to open?
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            The honest answer, at this stage, is: I think so — but I'm not done
            proving it yet.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-body">
            What I do know is that the research is solid, the design decisions
            are traceable, and the experience already feels different from
            everything else in this space. A tiny pixel fish as a reward for 25
            minutes of focus sounds absurd on paper. But there's something about
            it that works — the same way fishing itself works. You're not
            guaranteed to catch anything. But the possibility keeps you patient.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-signoff-line">
            That's the feeling Fishdoro is trying to bottle. The build
            continues. 🎣
          </p>
          <p class="cs-process-note">Process: Discover → Define → Develop</p>
        </section>

        <div style="height: 48px;"></div>
      </div><!-- /cs-inner -->
    </div><!-- /cs-content -->
  </div><!-- /cs-shell -->
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ── Shell layout ────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-shell {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    height: 100vh;
    background: #ffffff;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* ── Back nav ────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-back {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    padding: 14px 24px;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #6b8f4e;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 0.5px solid rgba(154, 175, 122, 0.2);
    flex-shrink: 0;
    transition: color 0.15s ease;
  }
  .cs-back:hover {
    color: #2d4a1e;
  }

  /* ── Progress bar ────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-progress-bar {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 2px;
    background: rgba(154, 175, 122, 0.15);
    z-index: 5;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .cs-progress-fill {
    height: 100%;
    background: #6b8f4e;
    width: 0%;
    transition: width 0.1s linear;
  }

  /* ── Scrollable content area ─────────────────────────── */
  .cs-content {
    flex: 1;
    overflow-y: auto;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    -ms-overflow-style: none;
  }
  .cs-content::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ── Inner width constraint ──────────────────────────── */
  .cs-inner {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 40px 24px;
  }

  /* ── Divider ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-divider {
    border: none;
    border-top: 0.5px solid rgba(154, 175, 122, 0.25);
    margin: 40px 0;
  }

  /* ── Section pill ────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-section-pill {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: #9aaf7a;
    background: rgba(200, 219, 160, 0.25);
    border: 1px solid rgba(107, 143, 78, 0.35);
    border-radius: 99px;
    padding: 3px 12px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  /* ── Section ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  .cs-section-title {
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: #2d4a1e;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-top: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ── Header ──────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-header {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* Tags */
  .cs-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .cs-tag {
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #4e7a30;
    background: rgba(200, 219, 160, 0.25);
    border: 0.5px solid rgba(107, 143, 78, 0.3);
    border-radius: 99px;
    padding: 3px 10px;
  }

  /* Title */
  .cs-title {
    font-family: "Playfair Display", Georgia, serif;
    font-size: 30px;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-style: italic;
    color: #2d4a1e;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  /* Subtitle */
  .cs-subtitle {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: #5a7040;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Body text */
  .cs-body {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: #5a7040;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* Background section — blockquote */
  .cs-blockquote {
    border-left: 2.5px solid rgba(107, 143, 78, 0.4);
    padding-left: 16px;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 300;
    font-style: italic;
    color: #5a7040;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Background section — subsection heading */
  .cs-subsection-title {
    font-family: "Inter", sans-serif;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: #2d4a1e;
    margin-top: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  /* Meta row */
  .cs-meta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 24px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding-top: 14px;
    border-top: 0.5px solid rgba(154, 175, 122, 0.25);
  }
  .cs-meta-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 2px;
  }
  .cs-meta-label {
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #9aaf7a;
  }
  .cs-meta-value {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #5a7040;
  }

  /* Hero image */
  .cs-hero-img {
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #e8e2d8;
    border: 0.5px solid rgba(154, 175, 122, 0.3);
  }
  .cs-hero-img img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 240px;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  /* ── Discover specifics ──────────────────────────────── */
  .cs-hint {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #9aaf7a;
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: -8px;
